.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt TEST 1
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm test ,
.Nm \&[
.Nd evaluate a condition
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar expression
.Nm \&[
.Ar expression Cm \&]
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
evaluates
.Ar expression
and exits 0 if it is true, 1 if it is false.
Called as
.Nm \&[ ,
its last argument must be
.Cm \&] .
.Ss Files
Each is true if
.Ar file
exists and:
.Bl -tag -width "-t fd" -compact
.It Fl b Ar file
is a block device
.It Fl c Ar file
is a character device
.It Fl d Ar file
is a directory
.It Fl e Ar file
(only exists)
.It Fl f Ar file
is a regular file
.It Fl g Ar file
has its set-group-ID bit set
.It Fl h Ar file
is a symbolic link; also
.Fl L
.It Fl k Ar file
has its sticky bit set
.It Fl p Ar file
is a named pipe
.It Fl r Ar file
is readable by this process
.It Fl s Ar file
is not empty
.It Fl S Ar file
is a socket
.It Fl u Ar file
has its set-user-ID bit set
.It Fl w Ar file
is writable by this process
.It Fl x Ar file
is executable (searchable, for a directory) by this process
.It Fl O Ar file
is owned by this process's effective user
.It Fl G Ar file
belongs to this process's effective group
.El
.Pp
.Fl t Ar fd
is true if descriptor
.Ar fd
is open on a terminal.
.Ar file1 Fl nt Ar file2
is true if
.Ar file1
was modified more recently than
.Ar file2 ,
or exists when
.Ar file2
doesn't;
.Fl ot
the reverse;
.Fl ef ,
if both names refer to the same file.
.Ss Strings and integers
.Bl -tag -width "s1 != s2" -compact
.It Ar string
not empty
.It Fl n Ar string
not empty
.It Fl z Ar string
empty
.It Ar s1 Cm = Ar s2
equal
.It Ar s1 Cm != Ar s2
not equal
.It Ar s1 Cm < Ar s2
sorts before (by byte value);
.Cm >
after
.It Ar n1 Fl eq Ar n2
the integers are equal; also
.Fl ne , gt , ge , lt , le
.El
.Ss Combining
.Bl -tag -width "e1 -o e2" -compact
.It Cm \&! Ar e
not
.It Ar e1 Fl a Ar e2
and, binding tighter than
.Fl o
.It Ar e1 Fl o Ar e2
or
.It Cm \&( Ar e Cm \&)
grouping
.El
.Pp
With up to four arguments, their number decides how they are read, as POSIX
specifies, so that for example
.Ql test -n
(one argument, a non-empty string) is true.
Write
.Fl a ,
.Fl o
and parentheses as separate arguments, and quote the parentheses for the shell.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 if the expression is true, 1 if false, 2 if it can't be evaluated.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
if [ -f /etc/rc.conf ] && [ "$x" -gt 3 ]; then ...
test \e( -d /tmp -o -d /var/tmp \e) -a -w /tmp
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr access 2 ,
.Xr stat 2
.Sh STANDARDS
.Nm
conforms to
.St -p1003.1-2008 ;
.Fl h ,
.Fl k ,
.Fl O ,
.Fl G ,
.Fl nt ,
.Fl ot ,
.Fl ef ,
.Cm <
and
.Cm >
are extensions, as on the BSDs.
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v7 .
